  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nWe have expanded the extreme heat warning over rest of central\nIL except having Vermilion, Edgar and Clark counties in a heat\nadvisory until 9 pm today. Heat indices this afternoon and early\nevening to peak from 105-115 with a few spots getting to 120 or\nhigher.\n\nAreas of fog have developed past few hours from Peoria to\nLincoln and Decatur to Robinson northeast, with dense fog\nrecently forming at Bloomington and Danville. A dense fog\nadvisory until 8 am from Livingston and LaSalle counties north.\nThe fog should lift and push northeast of central IL between 7-8\nam.\n\nMCS over western parts of TN/KY has a tail of convection\nextending northward into southeast IL (eastern Shelby, western\nCumberland, Effingham, Clay and western Jasper counties). Flash\nFlood warning for eastern Effingham county until 5 am and for\nsouthern Clay county until 6 am. A personal weather station on \nnorth side of Effingham measured 3.6 inches of rain at 315 am\nand wind gust 40 mph. Very high PWATs were 1.9-2.25 inches from\nDecatur SSE and cells moving SSE but backbuilding to the north \ntoward DEC airport. This boundary extends northward into DeWitt \nand western McLean county so may see some cells develop here too\nearly this morning. CAMs not handling this convection the best \nthough ARW is closer to reality and also Canadian REG latching \non to this convection longer. So this narrow band of convection \nmay last a few more hours until 13Z/8 am over parts of east \ncentral and especially southeast IL with heavy rain and frequent\nlightning the main threats. Though a few stronger cells could \nhave wind gusts over 40 mph. \n\nMLCAPEs currently 2500-4000 j/kg and MUCAPES are 4000-5000 j/kg\nover central/IL with a cap in place at dawn. This very unstable\nair mass to likely continue through the afternoon when CAPES\nlikely top 4000 j/kg and effective bulk shear values of 30-40\nkts. Challenging forecast today with convection chances since\ntriggers are not very obvious. Consensus of models was showing\nMCS developing over WI this morning and moving southward over\ncentral IL during this afternoon/early evening. Though this\nconvection has been slower to develop in east central WI. SPC\nDay1 outlook has upgraded to a slight risk over central and sw \nCWA and enhanced risk over ne CWA this afternoon/early evening. \nThere is 15-30% risk of damaging winds, with a level 1 for \nwinds of over 75 mph over much of CWA. There is a 2% to less\nthan 5% risk of tornadoes over CWA. The enhanced risk in ne CWA\nhas 5-15% risk of large hail. \n\nHighs today in the low to mid 90s combined with dewpoints in the\nupper 70s/lower 80s to give afternoon heat indices of 110-115 \nand few places reaching 120 or higher. Dewpoints got into the \nmid 80s late yesterday afternoon at SPI and Macomb with \nSpringfield 84 degrees setting a new record high dewpoint. \nWe expanded the extreme heat warning across rest of central IL\nexcept have heat advisory for Vermilion, Edgar and Clark\ncounties until 9 pm. If thunderstorms materialize and move in\nduring the afternoon and early evening, it would cool temps/heat\nindices and would likely end the heat headlines earlier than 9\npm. \n\nMain synoptic cold front to push southward across central IL\novernight and could produce isolated convection with better\nchances east of IL where better forcing expected. Muggy lows\ntonight in the low to mid 70s, warmest in southeast IL. Modest\nnortherly breezes on Tue bring in cooler and gradually less\nhumid air as dewpoints slip into the upper 60s/lower 70s Tue\nafternoon. Highs Tue in the mid to upper 80s with heat indices\nTue afternoon peaking in the mid to upper 80s from Springfield \nand Robinson sw. Quiet weather expected during mid week (Tue-\nThu) as weak high pressure ridge settles into IL on Wed with \nmore comfortable temperatures and humidity levels. Highs in the \nlow to mid 80s Wed and mid 80s Thu with lows in the low to mid \n60s Tue and Wed nights. \n\nMedium range models show a northern stream short wave trof\nmoving into IL on Friday into Saturday likely bringing next \nchance of showers and thunderstorms. Highs in the low to mid 80s\nFri/Sat and mid 80s Sunday. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for Aug \n3-9th has 50-60% chance of above normal temperatures over \ncentral and southern IL (45-50% chance above normal temps in \nnorthern IL) with precipitation trending near normal across IL.",
